  "worked_solution": "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-2$, $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function is neither left- nor right-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function is left-continuous but is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=0\\ne -1=f(0).\n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=2=f(1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=1$ only.",
